<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pompeius Trogus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Gnaeus Pompeius Trogus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gnaeus Pompeius Trogus was a Roman historian of the Augustan age from the Vocontii of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>, whose grandfather had received Roman citizenship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. His Philippic Histories, a universal history in 44 books centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and the Hellenistic kingdoms, is lost but survives in an abridgement by Justin, an important source for Hellenistic history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pompeius-trogus.jpg" alt="Pompeius Trogus" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pompeius_Trogus_1777_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pompeius Trogus was a Gallo-Roman historian from the Celtic Vocontii of Narbonese Gaul who lived under the emperor Augustus and was therefore a contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>. His grandfather served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> in his war against Sertorius and, through Pompey's influence, obtained Roman citizenship, so the family took their patron's praenomen and nomen, Gnaeus Pompeius, as was customary. Trogus's father served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> as his secretary and interpreter. Trogus seems to have been a polymath, and following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and Theophrastus he wrote books on the natural history of animals and plants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His principal work was the Philippic Histories and the Origin of the Whole World and the Places of the Earth, in 44 volumes. According to its surviving epitome, its main theme was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> founded by Philip II, but it functioned as a general history of the parts of the world that fell under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and his successors, with extensive ethnographical and geographical digressions. Trogus began with Ninus, the traditional founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, and ended at about the same point as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, in 9 CE. The development of the East from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> is covered extensively, while early Roman history and the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> are briefly treated in the last two books.</p><p>The work drew on earlier Greek historians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theopompus">Theopompus</a>, whose Philippica may have suggested the title, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a>, Timaeus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>. Because such a work was thought beyond the ability of a Gallo-Roman, it is generally assumed that he used a Greek compilation such as the Universal History of Timagenes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. Trogus criticised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> for putting elaborate speeches into the mouths of their characters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Philippic Histories is lost and survives in excerpts by other authors, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> and Augustine, and in a loose epitome by Justin, who preserved the parts he found most important or interesting. The last event Justin records is the recovery of Roman standards from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> in 20 BCE. Separate summaries of the books, the prologi, are preserved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of Justin. Even in this mutilated state the work is an important authority for the ancient history of the East. Trogus's books on animals and plants were quoted extensively by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>. His account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, set in the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, is, with the passages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, the most extensive description of the Jews in classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gnaeus Pompeius Trogus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The historian <b>Pompeius Trogus</b> came from a Vocontian family granted Roman citizenship.</blockquote><a href="/state/vocontii">Vocontii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaison-la-romaine">Vaison-la-Romaine</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.24,5.08&amp;year=-35" aria-label="Vaison-la-Romaine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4671 598 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pompeius Trogus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5121 868h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5121 868h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.24,5.08&amp;year=-35">What was here in 35 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
